WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.080
VIC IGLESIAS: Howdy, folks.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.250
My name's Vic Iglesias.

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.500
I'm a Cloud Solutions
Architect with Google Cloud.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.200
And today, I'd like to
walk through a architecture

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.542
of a GitOps based
CI/CD pipeline.

00:00:10.542 --> 00:00:12.000
When we talk about
CI/CD pipelines,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.880
we really have to think about
three different personas--

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.763
the developers, the operators,
and the security engineers.

00:00:17.763 --> 00:00:19.680
Our developers are going
to have their own Git

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.642
repository, where they store
their application code.

00:00:22.642 --> 00:00:24.100
That application
code is also going

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.670
to be able to kick off
continuous integration--

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.180
whenever you get a commit.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.820
And that continuous
integration job

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.038
is used to create an artifact.

00:00:32.038 --> 00:00:34.080
When we're talking about
deploying to Kubernetes,

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.530
our artifacts are
container images.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:39.030
So once continuous integration
creates that container image,

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.820
it's going to store it
in a container registry.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.580
That process is going
to go ahead and notify

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.940
another repository that
something has changed.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.490
In that repository,
we have our operators

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.740
creating configuration,
that then

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.950
says how our application is
actually going to be deployed.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:57.810
In order to manage
that configuration,

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.310
our Kubernetes
manifest, we're going

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.410
to use a tool called Customize.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:04.950
Customize is going to add in
that container image reference

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:09.900
into our config, and then commit
that to Kubernetes manifest

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.730
into a Git repository.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.410
That Git repository
stores the state

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.060
that we want to apply
into our environments.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.090
The first place where
we store that state

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:21.450
is within the staging
branch of this repository,

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.090
and that goes ahead
and deploys it straight

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.590
to our staging environment.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.050
Now, developers can
test out those changes,

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:29.688
see them live in a
staging environment.

00:01:29.688 --> 00:01:31.230
And once they're
happy, they're going

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:32.730
to tell the operators
that they want

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.860
to make a push to production--
or the operators can

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.533
decide to do that on their own.

00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:37.950
In order to do
that, they're going

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.590
to emerge from the staging
branch of this repository

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.240
into the master
branch, and that's

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.940
going to propagate those
changes down into the production

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:45.860
environment.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.360
Now, we haven't yet talked about
how we get those production

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.445
environments to be secure.

00:01:49.445 --> 00:01:50.820
The way we're
going to do that is

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:52.195
by letting the
security engineers

00:01:52.195 --> 00:01:53.740
have their own repository.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:56.010
And that repository is
going to store the state

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.720
that we're going to
use to apply policy

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.515
across all of our clusters.

00:01:59.515 --> 00:02:01.890
We're going to use Anthem's
config management to do that.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.380
We can store that state
in a repository that

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.750
has a structure that
allows us to apply

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.030
to select clusters
or all clusters

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:10.847
a certain set of configurations.

00:02:10.847 --> 00:02:12.930
For those security engineers,
those configurations

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.180
are going to be things like
role-based access control

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.690
policies, quotas for
various applications,

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:22.170
and things like restricting the
ability of containers to run

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:23.200
[INAUDIBLE].

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.070
So now they can make all of
those commits in an audible Git

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:29.040
repo, and then have them apply
to all of their production

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.640
infrastructure.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:31.833
Thank you for listening.

00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:34.250
Please come visit us at the
Google Cloud booth at Kubecon.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:37.600
[MUSIC PLAYING]

